{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1527605581,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MjgwNjIxMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2582#issuecomment-392806212"},"message":"@joeyh any hints on what could lead to `git-annex: failed to create OS thread: Cannot allocate memory?` on a linux system.  Michael said that issue happens only when we run git-annex, not when the same command ran in the terminal, so might be effects of us changing the environment (`PATH` to point to git annex standalone dir first, `GIT_SSH_COMMAND` and may be more)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1527609867,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MjgzMzQ1OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2582#issuecomment-392833458"},"message":"Seems likely to me that there's somehow a ulimit in place when datalad\nruns git-annex.\n\ngit-annex doesn't create any OS threads itself, only green threads,\nso this must be the ghc runtime, probably before git-annex runs at\nall.\n\nAlso, bear in mind that the ghc runtime currently allocates 1024 gb \nof memory, and relies on pages only being mapped by the kernel on\ndemand, rather than growing its memory region as needed. Which is \npretty weird, it didn't used to do that, but I have not seen it\ncause problems even with some ulimits.\n\n-- \nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527682119,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MzEzNjk4OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2582#issuecomment-393136989"},"message":"Turns out @joeyh pointed to the root of the issue. The machine in question had:\n\n```\n% cat /etc/security/limits.d/protect_headnode.conf\n*                hard    as            18000000\n```\nWith that removed, all works as expected. Something to watch out for with ghc, I guess.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527682119,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTY1MzM3NDAzMw=="},"status":2},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1527683319,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MzE0Mjg2Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2582#issuecomment-393142866"},"message":"Indeed it might be a showstopper for some hpc deployments","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1527691669,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MzE5MTE4Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2582#issuecomment-393191182"},"message":"Michael Hanke wrote:\n\u003e % cat /etc/security/limits.d/protect_headnode.conf\n\u003e *                hard    as            18000000\n\nSo a 170 gb address space limit and it still failed?\n\nI think this could legitimately be considered a bug in the ghc runtime\nif its preallocation trips such limits.\n\nBut, I tried to reproduce it and the git-annex commands I tried,\nincluding the more memory hungry ones like unused, were not affected.\nWhich makes sense since ghc preallocates 16 gb. \n\nWas this on 32 bit, Michael?\n\n-- \nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527692996,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MzE5OTU0Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2582#issuecomment-393199543"},"message":"No, not 32bit and plenty of real RAM also:\n\n```\n% uname -a\nLinux medusa 4.9.0-6-amd64 #1 SMP Debian 4.9.82-1+deb9u3 (2018-03-02) x86_64 GNU/Linux\n% free\n              total        used        free      shared  buff/cache   available\nMem:      264136204    28304728    14394228      486812   221437248   233481752\nSwap:     586194940     2485620   583709320\n```","files":null}]}